  "unresolved_identities": [
    {
      "id": "decoder_build.eckitlib_vendored_libs_record_mismatch",
      "description": "25 of 776 eckitlib RECORD entries, all under eckitlib.libs/ (auditwheel-vendored third-party runtime libraries: libcurl, libssl, libgssapi_krb5, libldap, libproj, libsqlite3, libssh and others), have on-disk SHA-256/size that does not match the dist-info RECORD. Observed size deltas range 3,368-213,008 bytes and are not uniform; per-file expected/observed hashes, sizes and deltas are in python_package_record_checks[eckitlib].mismatched_entry_details. No uniform post-packaging patch theory is supported by the captured bytes. Separately, an offline local pip-cache scan (see local_cached_wheel_comparison) found that all 25 installed payloads exactly equal an already-cached exact-version eckitlib wheel, and that cached wheel's own RECORD also disagrees with those 25 payloads -- local cached partial provenance, not independent upstream authentication, and not a benign-patch verdict. The exact cause and an authenticated original-reference byte set remain unresolved.",
      "status": "MISSING_EVIDENCE"
    },
    {
      "id": "decoder_build.no_direct_url_provenance",
      "description": "None of eccodes-2.48.0, eccodeslib-2.49.0.30, eckitlib-2.3.0.30 or findlibs-0.1.3 dist-info directories contain a direct_url.json, so the exact wheel source URL and upstream archive hash that produced this venv install cannot be independently confirmed from local metadata alone.",
      "status": "MISSING_EVIDENCE"
    },
    {
      "id": "decoder_build.memfs_definitions_samples_not_individually_attestable",
      "description": "eccodes.codes_definition_path() and codes_samples_path() both resolve to /MEMFS/definitions and /MEMFS/samples, an in-memory filesystem compiled directly into libeccodes_memfs.so (39767864 bytes, SHA-256 f984057845fd0a56... recorded above and consistent with its RECORD entry). No discrete definitions/samples files exist on disk to hash individually; only the containing library's own hash is attested. This is a containing-binary hash, not a fabricated per-definition manifest. Resolving individual embedded definition/sample identities would require either an independently reviewed source build of ecCodes with --disable-memfs (producing discrete filesystem artifacts to hash) or an offline, reviewed extraction/enumeration tool run against this exact library build, neither of which exists yet.",
      "status": "MISSING_EVIDENCE"
    },
    {
      "id": "decoder_build.no_lockfile_reproducible_build",
      "description": "The Alpha development venv has no checked-in lock file (e.g. requirements.txt/poetry.lock/pip-compile output) pinning eccodes/eccodeslib/eckitlib/findlibs versions; the repository's own requirements do not pin ecCodes (confirmed in the prior source assessment). Rebuilding an equivalent venv from the repository alone is not currently reproducible without this inventory.",
      "status": "MISSING_EVIDENCE"
    },
    {
      "id": "decoder_build.native_dependency_graph_wider_than_decode_need",
      "description": "Importing eccodes in this venv eagerly loads eckitlib, which pulls in libcurl, libssl, libgssapi_krb5, libldap, libssh, libsqlite3 and libproj even for a purely local, in-memory GRIB decode with no network or database use. This expands the native attack/trust surface beyond what the Gate 3 decode path requires; independent review should assess whether a narrower ecCodes build (without eckit's networked backends) is available or required. Separately, loaded_native_libraries (42 entries) is itself only a path-substring filter over /proc/self/maps (substrings: eccodes, eckit, libaec, openjp2, libpng), not a complete native dependency graph of the process: an independent pass over this same process identified 36 additional loaded shared objects outside that filter, including NumPy/OpenBLAS (libscipy_openblas64_, libgfortran, libquadmath, numpy _multiarray_umath/_umath_linalg), the CFFI backend (_cffi_backend), Python stdlib dynload extension modules, system/libc shared objects (ld-linux, libc, libstdc++, libssl.so.3, libcrypto.so.3, libsqlite3.so.0.8.6, libz, libbz2, liblzma, libexpat, libffi, libgcc_s, libdl, libpthread, libresolv, librt), and -- only in a repair/review run with process-level network-denial containment -- libseccomp.so.2.5.5, which is containment-only and never a production dependency. None of the 36 are claimed here as production decoder dependencies; whether each is interpreter overhead, a NumPy/test-only load, or a genuine production dependency is unresolved and would require a before/after loaded-library map to distinguish.",
      "status": "OBSERVATION_FOR_REVIEW"
    },
    {
      "id": "decoder_build.realistic_resource_qualification_not_performed",
      "description": "Observed peak RSS (~88,456-88,484 KB) and CPU time are diagnostics from a tiny bounded synthetic decode (one template-0 message plus two re-encoded CCSDS messages), not a realistic-field-size resource measurement, and are not compared here against any Gate 3 memory-floor acceptance threshold. A decoder peak-memory measurement under realistic (non-synthetic) field sizes, and its comparison against the G3-L resource floor, remains a separate, later, independently-reviewed task.",
      "status": "MISSING_EVIDENCE"
    }
  ],
  "disclaimer": "UNREVIEWED_BUILD_OBSERVATION / NOT_QUALIFIED. This is a build-byte inventory of the already-installed decoder dependencies, not a repeat of the Gate 3 decoder/source assessment and not a qualification or launch verdict. G3-L remains NO-GO. No provider request, concrete transport wiring or CCSDS enablement is authorized by this document. 91/200 formal 1/50 NOT_READY_TO_FUND."
